From: Marionette <marionette@localhost>
Subject: [EXPERIMENTAL] make capture_hold=0 actually release the monitor filters

marionette_monitor_refresh_filters() installed the monitor filters
unconditionally:

	dp_mon_filter_setup_mon_mode_1_0(pdev);
	return dp_mon_filter_update_1_0(pdev);

so writing marionette_awdl_capture_hold=0 re-armed exactly what it was supposed
to release.  Normal RX never came back while the monitor vdev existed, the
station missed beacons, and policy manager dropped the link after a run of them:

	lim_ps_offload_handle_missed_beacon_ind: Received Heart Beat Failure
	wlan0(vdevid-0): ... QCA_DISCONNECT_REASON_BEACON_MISS_FAILURE

Measured 2026-10-23, all with the station associated to channel 52:

| shape                                   | station | AWDL frames |
|-----------------------------------------|---------|-------------|
| vdev resident, filters never held        | alive   | 0 (expected)|
| vdev resident, filters toggled 2s/3s     | dead 10s| stops       |
| vdev resident, filters held + ROC rearm  | dead 15s| stops       |
| vdev churned (2 s window, delete after)  | alive   | flowing     |

Only deleting the vdev restored normal RX, which forced the service to churn
vdevs.  Every vdev delete costs exactly one

	target_if_vdev_mgr_rsp_timer_start: DELETE requested, waiting for STOP response
	QDF ASSERT in target_if_vdev_mgr_assert_mgmt

one assert per window regardless of a 3 s or 12 s gap, because the firmware's
STOP response is never accounted for.  Over ~10 minutes that accumulation took
the whole device down twice.

This patch makes the release path do what the detach path does: reset the
monitor mode before updating, so the shared RXDMA ring returns to its normal
interpretation and beacons keep arriving.  The capture phase is now passed
explicitly instead of being ignored.

If this works, the resident design needs no vdev churn at all: one vdev opened
once, filters toggled, no asserts, no reboots.

--- a/qca-wifi-host-cmn/dp/wifi3.0/monitor/1.0/dp_mon_filter_1.0.c
+++ b/qca-wifi-host-cmn/dp/wifi3.0/monitor/1.0/dp_mon_filter_1.0.c
@@ -1047,7 +1047,7 @@ QDF_STATUS dp_mon_filter_update_1_0(struct dp_pdev *pdev)
 
 /* Called only with the monitor vdev operation guard held by the bounded
  * experiment. Never cache the SoC or queue work across teardown. */
-int marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(void *soc_handle)
+int marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(void *soc_handle, bool hold)
 {
 	struct dp_soc *soc = soc_handle;
 	struct dp_pdev *pdev;
@@ -1059,7 +1059,24 @@ int marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(void *soc_handle)
 	    !pdev->monitor_pdev->monitor_configured ||
 	    !pdev->monitor_pdev->mvdev)
 		return QDF_STATUS_E_INVAL;
-	dp_mon_filter_setup_mon_mode_1_0(pdev);
+	/* Releasing must actually release.
+	 *
+	 * This used to install the monitor filters unconditionally, so writing
+	 * capture_hold=0 re-armed them and normal RX never came back: the
+	 * station missed beacons and policy manager dropped the link after a
+	 * run of them (QCA_DISCONNECT_REASON_BEACON_MISS_FAILURE, ~10 s).
+	 * Only deleting the monitor vdev restored normal RX, which is why the
+	 * service had to churn vdevs -- and every vdev delete costs one
+	 * target_if_vdev_mgr assertion because the firmware's STOP response is
+	 * never accounted for.
+	 *
+	 * Resetting the monitor mode puts the shared RXDMA ring back to its
+	 * normal interpretation, which is what the detach path does.
+	 */
+	if (!hold)
+		dp_mon_filter_reset_mon_mode_1_0(pdev);
+	else
+		dp_mon_filter_setup_mon_mode_1_0(pdev);
 	return dp_mon_filter_update_1_0(pdev);
 }
 
--- a/platform/inc/marionette_sta_monitor.h
+++ b/platform/inc/marionette_sta_monitor.h
@@ -4,5 +4,5 @@
 #include <linux/types.h>
 bool marionette_sta_monitor_enabled(void);
 bool marionette_sta_monitor_capture_enabled(void);
-int marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(void *soc_handle);
+int marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(void *soc_handle, bool hold);
 #endif
--- a/qcacld-3.0/core/hdd/src/wlan_hdd_main.c
+++ b/qcacld-3.0/core/hdd/src/wlan_hdd_main.c
@@ -20053,7 +20053,7 @@ static int marionette_capture_once_set(const char *value,
 	if (!target_freq)
 		target_freq = home_freq;
 	WRITE_ONCE(marionette_capture_phase, true);
-	capture_status = marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(soc);
+	capture_status = marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(soc, true);
 	if (QDF_IS_STATUS_SUCCESS(capture_status)) {
 		if (target_freq != home_freq)
 			tune_status = wlan_hdd_set_mon_chan(adapter, target_freq,
@@ -20065,7 +20065,7 @@ static int marionette_capture_once_set(const char *value,
 	if (target_freq != home_freq)
 		home_status = wlan_hdd_set_mon_chan(adapter, home_freq, home_width);
 	WRITE_ONCE(marionette_capture_phase, false);
-	restore_status = marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(soc);
+	restore_status = marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(soc, false);
 	hdd_info("MARIONETTE_CAPTURE_ONCE freq=%u home=%u ms=%u capture=%d tune=%d home_restore=%d rx_restore=%d",
 		 target_freq, home_freq, duration, capture_status, tune_status,
 		 home_status, restore_status);
@@ -20157,7 +20157,7 @@ static int marionette_capture_hold_set(const char *value,
 		goto stop;
 	}
 	WRITE_ONCE(marionette_capture_phase, hold);
-	status = marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(soc);
+	status = marionette_monitor_refresh_filters(soc, hold);
 	hdd_info("MARIONETTE_CAPTURE_HOLD hold=%d status=%d", hold, status);
 	ret = qdf_status_to_os_return(status);
 stop:
